<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    A class library, never the web SDK: this layer persists and secures, it does
    not host HTTP. No ASP.NET Core package and no framework reference to the
    ASP.NET Core shared framework may be added here.

    The reference on Application is deliberate: some of the abstractions this
    layer implements are declared there, and its options classes are bound here
    through IOptions<T>. Never reference DnnMigration.Api, which already
    references this project and would invert the graph, and never reference a test
    project or a legacy project under Library/ or Website/.

    This project owns every Entity Framework Core, SQL client, password-hashing,
    health-check and token-signing reference in the solution, and nothing else.
    Swagger, versioning and logging belong to Api; validation belongs to
    Application; the test database providers belong to the integration test
    project, because shipping one here would put a test provider into production.

    JWT is split across two projects because minting and validating a token are
    different jobs in different layers. This project mints — JwtTokenService signs
    the access token — so it references the signing library. Api validates every
    inbound token on the request path, which is middleware, so it references the
    ASP.NET Core bearer authentication handler. Moving either reference to the
    other project forces one of those responsibilities into a layer that must not
    hold it.

    Build policy is inherited from backend/Directory.Build.props, which is why
    this file declares no PropertyGroup. Restating or overriding an inherited
    property would weaken the Release build's warnings-as-errors policy.
  -->

  <ItemGroup>

    <!--
      The 8.0.29 band matches the installed shared framework exactly. Every
      version here is exact-pinned: no floating range, wildcard or moving tag.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore" Version="8.0.29" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Relational" Version="8.0.29" />

    <!--
      Provider for the existing, unaltered SQL Server database. The legacy
      provider registration uses an empty object qualifier and "dbo" as the
      database owner, so those are the defaults entity configurations bind to.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.SqlServer" Version="8.0.29" />

    <!--
      Keep this reference PLAIN. Adding PrivateAssets="all", the shape the stock
      template uses, stops the design-time library flowing to referencing
      projects. The EF CLI resolves design-time services from the startup project,
      and the API project receives this package only transitively from here, so
      severing the flow breaks migration tooling with the familiar "startup
      project does not reference Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Design" error. The
      separate command-line tooling package is not referenced; dotnet-ef is
      installed as a global tool.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Design" Version="8.0.29" />

    <!--
      DIRECT PIN, AND IT MUST STAY DIRECT. Without it the SQL Server provider
      resolves 5.1.7 transitively through EF Core. A direct PackageReference wins
      resolution outright, so it is also what stops a future EF Core patch moving
      the client underneath the solution.

      5.2.3 is the version the frozen dependency inventory names, and raising a
      pinned major here alone would mean building a graph other than the one that
      was reviewed and that the lock files assert. Change the inventory first,
      then this line.

      Known trade-off: NuGet's DEPRECATION listing flags identities inside 5.2.3's
      Entra ID chain (Azure.Identity 1.11.4 and the Microsoft.IdentityModel 6.35.0
      family). Deprecation carries no NU diagnostic, and that chain is the
      interactive/Entra authentication path this solution does not use — every
      connection string in the delivery authenticates with a SQL login. The
      vulnerability position, which the warnings-as-errors policy can observe, is
      clean: restoring with NuGetAudit at mode=all and level=low reports no
      advisory against 5.2.3 or anything it brings in. A deployment that needs
      Entra ID authentication raises the client version in the inventory and
      re-verifies this project's audit position in the same change.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Data.SqlClient" Version="5.2.3" />

    <!--
      One-way password hashing, replacing a reversibly-encrypted credential store
      whose key was committed to the legacy configuration. The migration-only
      LegacyCredentialVerifier performs bounded, opt-in comparison of legacy rows;
      AuthService immediately replaces an accepted value through this package's
      one-way BCrypt implementation, with administrative reset as the fallback.
      Security/BcryptPasswordHasher.cs uses this package's ENHANCED hash and
      verify pair, which pre-digests the credential and so removes the plain
      algorithm's 72-byte input-equivalence behaviour; the two halves must always
      be changed together. The legacy password policy is preserved rather than
      tightened mid-migration. Both divergences are recorded in
      MIGRATION_NOTES.md.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="BCrypt.Net-Next" Version="4.0.3" />

    <!--
      Referenced for the health-check ABSTRACTIONS, not for its own SQL Server
      check. This project is a plain class library with no framework reference to
      draw Microsoft.Extensions.Diagnostics.HealthChecks from, so this package is
      the sole route by which IHealthCheck, HealthCheckResult, HealthStatus,
      AddHealthChecks() and AddCheck<T>() reach HealthChecks/DatabaseHealthCheck.cs
      and DependencyInjection.cs. Removing it breaks the compile.

      AddSqlServer() is deliberately NOT called: it reads the same
      ConnectionStrings:Default value and opens a connection to the same instance
      as this project's own probe, so registering both would put two connections
      behind every poll of a continuously probed path while distinguishing
      nothing. The endpoint it serves must stay anonymous and reachable, because
      docker-compose gates the frontend service on the API becoming healthy.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="AspNetCore.HealthChecks.SqlServer" Version="8.0.2" />

    <!--
      ACCESS-TOKEN SIGNING FOR JwtTokenService, THROUGH THE ONE JWT IDENTITY THE
      DEPENDENCY INVENTORY NAMES. JwtBearer 8.0.29 resolves
      Microsoft.IdentityModel.Protocols.OpenIdConnect 7.7.3, which brings
      System.IdentityModel.Tokens.Jwt 7.7.3 and Microsoft.IdentityModel.Tokens
      7.7.3 — the same band the Api project's own JwtBearer reference resolves. So
      the assembly that signs a token here and the assembly that validates it on
      the request path are one loaded version, and a signature that verifies in a
      unit test verifies in production. Referencing the token library directly
      instead would compile against the 6.35.0 band the data provider carries
      while running against 7.7.3.

      The cost is stated rather than glossed: this reference brings a framework
      reference to Microsoft.AspNetCore.App with it. Nothing in this project
      touches the request pipeline and nothing may start doing so — authentication
      handlers, middleware and endpoint plumbing belong to the Api layer, and the
      Domain project remains free of every package. Bump this and the Api's
      JwtBearer reference together or neither.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authentication.JwtBearer" Version="8.0.29" />

  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\DnnMigration.Domain\DnnMigration.Domain.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\DnnMigration.Application\DnnMigration.Application.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    ONE FRIEND ASSEMBLY, NAMED EXPLICITLY, AND NOTHING WIDENED TO PUBLIC FOR A TEST.

    Everything this layer implements is reachable through a PUBLIC contract — IPasswordHasher,
    IClock, ICacheService, IHostSettingsService, IPortalContext and IRefreshTokenStore from
    Domain, ITokenService and IPermissionEvaluator from Application, the nine repository
    interfaces and IUnitOfWork from Domain — and every one of them is registered by
    AddInfrastructure. That is the preferred route and the one the integration suites take:
    DnnMigration.IntegrationTests/Security/ resolves the real password hasher, token service,
    permission evaluator and refresh-token store from the composed container, which proves the
    registration as well as the behaviour. Nothing here should be reached by `new` when the
    container can hand it over.

    The grant below exists for the components whose behaviour is asserted with SUBSTITUTED
    COLLABORATORS rather than through the container: LoggingAuditSink, LegacyCredentialVerifier,
    JwtTokenService, DatabaseHealthCheck, RefreshTokenStore, ModuleBusinessControllerFactory and the
    persistence translators are each internal sealed, and each is covered by a suite that hands it a
    recording logger, a mutable clock or a fabricated fault and asserts what it does with them. Those
    facts are not reachable from a resolved instance, because the substitution IS the fact; what they
    need is the type.

    ⚠ THE GRANT NAMES DnnMigration.IntegrationTests, AND ONLY THAT ASSEMBLY. The unit project has a
    single edge to Application, so a suite there cannot name an Infrastructure type at all; the
    integration project reaches this assembly through its own single edge to the API. Do not add a
    second name, in particular not DnnMigration.UnitTests, do not grant to a wildcard, and do not
    widen a type from internal to public to make a test compile — including DnnDbContext, whose
    internal declaration is what keeps Application away from the model.

    The production layering guarantee is a property of the four PRODUCTION project files rather than
    of this item: Domain declares zero project references, Application cannot see Infrastructure, and
    either violation remains a compile error. A test project is the outermost consumer of the graph
    rather than a layer within it.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="DnnMigration.IntegrationTests" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
